 must ensure that the solution obtained, in the previous addition, is a clear solution before proceeding to add the next solvent. Physical methods such\nL238: as vortex, ultrasound or hot water bath can be used to aid dissolving.\nL239: ## Chemical Information, Storage & Stability\nL240: \nL241: Molecular Weight  | 361.03  | Formula  | C_{14}H_{19}BCl_{2}N_{2}O_{4}  | Storage (From the date of receipt)  | 3 years-20°C powder\nL242: CAS No.  | 1072833-77-2  | cite58†Download SDF†file.selleckchem.com | Storage of Stock Solutions  | 1 year-80°C in solvent\nL243: 1 month-20°C in solvent\nL244: Synonyms  | N/A  | Smiles  | B(C(CC(C)C)NC(=O)CNC(=O)C1=C(C=CC(=C1)Cl)Cl)(O)O\nL245: ## Mechanism of Action\nL246: Features  | A first-in-class proteasome inhibitor that has improved pharmacokinetics (PK), pharmacodynamics(PD), and antitumor activity in preclinical studies.\nL247: Targets/IC_{50}/Ki  | 20S proteasome [Button: [1]]\nL248: (Cell-free assay) 0.93 nM(Ki) 20S proteasome [Button: [1]]\nL249: (Cell-free assay) 3.4 nM\nL250: In vitro  | At higher concentrations, this compound also inhibits the caspase-like (β1) and trypsin-like (β2) proteolytic sites with IC50 of 31nM and 3.5uM, respectively. It inhibits Calu-6 cell with IC50 of 9.7 nM. MLN2238 is a selective, potent, and reversible inhibitor of the proteasome in tumor cells. This compound shows time-dependent reversible proteasome inhibition.\nL251: Both this compound and Bortezomib shows time-dependent reversible proteasome inhibition; however, the proteasome dissociation half-life for it is determined to be ∼6-fold faster than that of Bortezomib (18 and 110 minutes, respectively). It dissociates more rapidly from the proteasome than Bortezomib, consistent with faster recovery of proteasome activity observed in the Proteasome-Glo assay. It has a greater overall tumor pharmacodynamic effect than Bortezomib as assessed by 20S inhibition.\nL252: [Button: [1]]This compound is the biologically active form of MLN9708. [Button: [2]]\nL253: Kinase Assay  | Kinase assay\nL254: Calu-6 cells are cultured in MEM containing 10% fetal bovine serum and 1% penicillin/streptomycin and plated 1 day before the start of the experiment at 1 × 10^{4} cells per well in a 384-well plate. Proteasome activity is assessed by monitoring hydrolysis of the chymotrypsin-like substrate Suc-LLVY-aminoluciferin in the presence of luciferase using the Proteasome-Glo assay reagents according to the manufacturer's instructions. Luminescence is measured using a LEADseeker instrument.\nL255: In vivo  | MLN2238 induces a greater pharmacodynamic response than Bortezomib in xenograft tumors. This compound shows greater maximum and sustained tumor proteasome inhibition compared with Bortezomib in xenograft models. These results confirm that the improved tumor exposure seen with this agent translates into an improved tumor pharmacodynamic response both at the level of and downstream from the proteasome. It shows antitumor activity in the CWR22 xenograft model.\nL256: This chemical shows greater tumor pharmacodynamic responses in WSU-DLCL2 xenografts compared with Bortezomib. Similarly, Bortezomib treatment only led to a minor increase in GADD34 levels in WSU-DLCL2 xenograft tumors, whereas it strongly induces its expression. [Button: [1]] This compound has an improved pharmacodynamic profile and antitumor activity compared with Bortezomib in both OCI-Ly10 and PHTX22L models. [Button: [2]]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMLN2238 (Ixazomib) | Proteasome inhibitor | 50+ Citations | In Stock | Selleck Chemicals (https://www.selleckchem.com/products/MLN-2238.html)\nciteturn217view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn216view3\",\"pattern\":\"S2180\"}); Total lines: 400\nL77: # MLN2238 (Ixazomib) Proteasome inhibitor\nL78: \nL79: Cat.No.S2180\nL80: \nL81: Ixazomib (MLN2238) inhibits the chymotrypsin-like proteolytic (β5) site of the 20S proteasome with IC50 and K_{i} of 3.4 nM and 0.93 nM in cell-free assays, respectively, also inhibits the caspase-like (β1) and trypsin-like (β2) proteolytic sites, with IC50 of 31 and 3500 nM. Ixazomib (MLN2238) induces autophagy. Phase 3.\nL82: \nL83: cite46†Image: MLN2238 (Ixazomib) Proteasome inhibitor Chemical Structure†file.selleckchem.com L84: \nL85: Chemical Structure\nL86: ## Molecular Weight: 361.03\nL87: \nL88: Jump to [Button: Mechanism of Action] [Button: Cell Culture & Working Concentration] [Button: Solubility] [Button: Chemical Information, Storage & Stability] [Button: Specificity]\nL254: Calu-6 cells are cultured in MEM containing 10% fetal bovine serum and 1% penicillin/streptomycin and plated 1 day before the start of the experiment at 1 × 10^{4} cells per well in a 384-well plate. Proteasome activity is assessed by monitoring hydrolysis of the chymotrypsin-like substrate Suc-LLVY-aminoluciferin in the presence of luciferase using the Proteasome-Glo assay reagents according to the manufacturer's instructions. Luminescence is measured using a LEADseeker instrument.\nL255: In vivo  | MLN2238 induces a greater pharmacodynamic response than Bortezomib in xenograft tumors. This compound shows greater maximum and sustained tumor proteasome inhibition compared with Bortezomib in xenograft models. These results confirm that the improved tumor exposure seen with this agent translates into an improved tumor pharmacodynamic response both at the level of and downstream from the proteasome. It shows antitumor activity in the CWR22 xenograft model.\nL256: This chemical shows greater tumor pharmacodynamic responses in WSU-DLCL2 xenografts compared with Bortezomib. Similarly, Bortezomib treatment only led to a minor increase in GADD34 levels in WSU-DLCL2 xenograft tumors, whereas it strongly induces its expression. [Button: [1]] This compound has an improved pharmacodynamic profile and antitumor activity compared with Bortezomib in both OCI-Ly10 and PHTX22L models. [Button: [2]]\nL257: References  |\nL258:   * cite59†[1] https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20160034/†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L259:   * [2] https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21903769/\nL260: ## Applications\nL261: \nL262: Methods  | Biomarkers  | Images  | PMID\nL263: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL264: Western blot  | PARP / Cleaved PARP / Caspase-3 / Cleaved Caspase-3 Mcl-1 / Bcl-2 p53 / p21 / NOXA / PUMA / pRb / E2F / Cyclin D1 / CDK6  | cite60†Image: S2180-WB1†file.selleckchem.com | 27687684\nL265: Growth inhibition assay  | IC50  | cite61†Image: S2180-viability1†file.selleckchem.com | 29416618\nL266: ## Clinical Trial Information\nL267: \nL268: (data from cite62†https://clinicaltrials.gov†clinicaltrials.gov , updated on 2024-05-22)\nL269: \nL270: NCT Number  | Recruitment  | Conditions  | Sponsor/Collaborators  | Start Date  | Phases\nL271: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL272: cite63†NCT00963820†clinicaltrials.gov | Completed  | Multiple Myeloma  | Millennium Pharmaceuticals Inc.|Takeda  | October 2009  | Phase 1\nL273: ## Tech Support\nL274: \nL275: cite64†Handling Instructions†file.selleckchem.com L276: \nL277: Tel: +1-832-582-8158 Ext:3\nL278: \nL279: If you have any other enquiries, please leave a message.\nL280: \nL281: [Input]\nL282: \nL283: * Name [Input] Please enter your name.\nL284: \nL285: * Email [Input] Please enter your email. Please enter a valid email address.\nL286: \nL287: * Comments Please write something to us.\nL288: \nL289: [Button: Submit]\nL290: ## Signaling Pathway Map\nL291: \nL292: cite65†iframe L293: \nL294: Selleck\nL295: \nL296:   * cite66†About Us L297:   * cite67†Careers L298:   * cite68†News/Events/Press Release L299: \nL300: Support\nL301: \nL302:   * cite69†Tech Support & FAQs L303:   * cite70†Quality Control L304:   * cite71†Shipping & Delivery L305: \nL306: Legal\nL307: \nL308:   * cite72†Terms & Conditions L309:   * cite73†Privacy Policy L310: \nL311: cite74†Image: